.TH "cbor::item_size" "3" "2026-11-08" "mbedded\-cbor 0.9.0" "mbedded\-cbor"
.SH NAME
cbor::item_size \- checks the first item and gives its size in bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<cbor/cbor.hpp>\f[R]
.EE
.IP
.EX
std::expected<std::size_t, error> item_size(std::string_view encoded);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Gives the size in bytes of the first item in \f[CR]encoded\f[R].
Checks that the item is well\-formed.
Bytes after the item do not change the result.
The call does not recurse: an item of any depth gives its size.
.PP
\f[B]Parameters\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]encoded\f[R]: the bytes that start with the item
.SH RETURN VALUE
the size of the item in bytes
.SH ERRORS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R]: the bytes end before the item ends
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]syntax_error\f[R]: a head is not well\-formed
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]indefinite_length\f[R]: a head uses indefinite length
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]string_length_exceeded\f[R]: a string is longer than
\f[CR]limits::string_length\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]container_elements_exceeded\f[R]: the count of an array or a map
is greater than \f[CR]limits::container_elements\f[R]
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// bytes: [1, 2] followed by 7, 4 bytes\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const size = cbor::item_size(bytes);                              \f[I]// 3\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const cut = cbor::item_size(std::string_view(bytes).substr(0, 2)); \f[I]// too_little_data\f[R]
\f[I]// [_ 1]\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const open = cbor::item_size(\(dq\(rsx9f\(rsx01\(rsxff\(dqsv);                   \f[I]// indefinite_length\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
mbedded\-cbor(3), cbor::lazy(3), cbor::at_path(3), cbor::item(3), cbor::structs(3), cbor::schema(3), cbor::ref(3), cbor::sequence(3), cbor::sequence_reader(3), cbor::diagnostic_notation(3), cbor::error(3)
